22:46:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Bats are not completely blind—that’s a myth. Most bats have functional eyes and some even have good vision, especially in low light. However, to navigate and hunt insects in total darkness, they rely primarily on **echolocation** (or biosonar). Here’s how it works: - A bat emits high-pitched sound pulses, usually through its mouth or nose. - These sounds are ultrasonic—too high for humans to hear—and they bounce off objects in the environment. - The bat’s sensitive ears catch the returning echoes, and its brain processes the time delay, loudness, and frequency shifts to create a detailed auditory “picture” of its surroundings. - This allows the bat to detect, track, and capture tiny flying insects with pinpoint precision, even in complete darkness. So, instead of seeing with light, they “see” with sound. Some species can even adjust the pitch and pattern of their calls when closing in on prey to get more detailed information—a behavior called the “terminal buzz.”
